# Refresh the apt package cache. **Action-shaped** (ADR-0029): it always acts, and says so.
#
# It used to claim convergence when an `*Release` file under /var/lib/apt/lists had been
# modified in the last hour. That question cannot be answered from a mtime: apt sends a
# conditional request and, on `304 Not Modified`, leaves the file alone; when it does
# refetch, it preserves the repository's `Last-Modified`. The timestamp therefore records
# **when Debian last published**, not when this host last refreshed. Measured minutes apart
# on a live target: `apt-get update`, and the mtime stayed at 12:57 while the clock said
# 14:02. (`-name '*Release'` did not match `…_InRelease` either — two independent reasons
# for the same permanent non-convergence.)
#
# So the observe was not merely imprecise, it could essentially never be satisfied: the def
# acted on every run while reporting a convergence it had never reached. Rather than make
# the window configurable — which would only expose a timestamp that does not mean what it
# appears to mean — the def stops pretending to have a desired state (#488).
#
# Gate it the way handlers are gated: `if repo.changed { apt.update() }`, the pattern
# `service.restart` already uses. A cache refresh is an action, and the plan is the only
# thing that knows whether it is wanted.
def update() as root {
    apply {
        r = shell { apt-get update }
        if !r { return err.runtime(r) }
        return ok.updated
    }
}
